    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================
         Day 13x In-Class.  Week 7 session 3 (plans/day13x-prompt.md)
         fills this section per plans/day13x.md: the proof-mass physics,
         MEMS, the datasheet characteristics table, the data format
         (two's complement is FIRST TEACHING here), auto-increment and
         byte assembly, the control registers.  x-day: no reading.

         PARKED below (commented out): the salvageable pieces of the
         rough chapter, none of them trusted (ground truth §7).  Figures
         are rebuilt per the manifest (ground truth §6), not reused as
         extracted.
         ============================================================ -->
    <section xml:id="sec-accel-day13x">
        <title>Day 13x In-Class: What a MEMS Accelerometer Is</title>

        <!-- DELIVERY 2: section introduction prose.  OWES: the day's opening
             paragraph (yesterday we read 0x33 out of a chip without knowing
             what is inside it; today is the inside — the physics, the
             datasheet's numbers, and the format the data arrives in
             tomorrow), in the shape of sec-accel-day13's introduction. -->

        <!-- Part 1 -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day13x-physics">
            <title>Part 1: The Physics of a Proof Mass</title>

            <!-- DELIVERY 2: prose.  OWES: the lead-in paragraph naming the
                 proof mass and the spring (the level-app hook lives in
                 act-day13x-mass-commit's introduction and is not repeated);
                 the derivation paragraph following sl-day13x-derivation
                 (F = ma and F = kx as two named facts, the same force, so
                 kx = ma and a = (k/m)x; one axis per spring, three copies
                 for three axes); the displacement-to-capacitance paragraph
                 beside fig-accel-capacitive (resistive/capacitive/inductive
                 options, we use capacitive); and the MEMS paragraph beside
                 fig-accel-mems (machined out of silicon; the
                 capacitance-to-voltage circuit; the C1/C2 differential
                 detail is Reference material, sec-accel-reference — do not
                 grow it back here). -->

            <activity xml:id="act-day13x-mass-commit">
                <title>What does the mass do?</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>Your phone already measures what we are wiring up this
                    week: open its level app and tilt the phone, and the
                    reading follows.  Something inside the phone senses the
                    motion, and today we look at how.  Inside the sensor
                    a small mass — the <term>proof mass</term> — is connected
                    by a spring to the sensor's case.</p>
                </introduction>
                <task xml:id="task-day13x-mass-commit">
                    <statement><p>Suppose the case accelerates to the right.
                    Seen from inside the case, what does the mass do?  Commit
                    to an answer, and to a reason, before we work it
                    out.</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>

            <instructor xml:id="ins-day13x-mass-commit">
                <p>Because of its inertia, the mass gets left behind: seen
                from inside the case, it moves backward — opposite the
                acceleration — and the spring stretches until the spring
                force matches the force needed to carry the mass along with
                the case.  That displacement is the measurement.  How far the
                spring stretches, and what it has to do with the size of the
                acceleration, is the derivation we do next.</p>
            </instructor>

            <figure xml:id="fig-accel-proof-mass">
                <caption>A proof mass <m>m</m> connected by a spring (spring
                constant <m>k</m>) to the sensor's case.  When the case
                accelerates, the mass is displaced by <m>x</m> from its rest
                position; at balance the spring force <m>kx</m> equals the
                force <m>ma</m> that accelerates the mass with the case, so
                measuring <m>x</m> gives the acceleration
                <m>a = (k/m)\,x</m>.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day13x-Accelerometer/mass_spring.svg" width="72%"/>
            </figure>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13x-derivation" ref="fig-accel-proof-mass">
    <ul>
        <li>Acceleration <m>a</m> results in force <m>ma</m> on the mass — that is the force it takes to carry the mass along with the case.</li>
        <li>The spring counteracts with force <m>kx</m>, where <m>k</m> is the spring constant and <m>x</m> is the displacement.</li>
        <li>These are the same force, so <m>kx = ma</m> — and <m>a = (k/m)x</m>: if we observe a displacement <m>x</m>, then the mass has undergone acceleration <m>a</m>.</li>
        <li>This system only measures acceleration along the length of the spring.  For a 3-axis accelerometer, duplicate it along three axes.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>≈ 4 min, the reveal's derivation — her slide 3, two named facts before they are combined. If we can measure the displacement we can compute acceleration; the whole rest of today is the two hand-offs that follow (displacement to capacitance, capacitance to bits). No physics course is assumed — both facts are named on the slide.</note>
</slide>

            <figure xml:id="fig-accel-capacitive">
                <caption>Turning displacement into something we can measure
                electrically: one capacitor plate is attached to the mass
                and one to the case.  As the mass moves, the gap between
                the plates changes, and the capacitance changes with
                it.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day13x-Accelerometer/capacitive_pickoff.svg" width="62%"/>
            </figure>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13x-capacitive" ref="fig-accel-capacitive">
    <ul>
        <li>There are multiple ways of measuring a displacement: resistive, capacitive, and inductive techniques.  Our sensor uses the capacitive one.</li>
        <li>Imagine capacitor plates attached to the mass and to the case: as the mass moves, the gap changes, and measuring the capacitance lets us compute <m>x</m>.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>≈ 1 min. Her slide 4. The chain so far: acceleration → displacement → capacitance. One hand-off left: capacitance → a number.</note>
</slide>

            <figure xml:id="fig-accel-mems">
                <caption>The whole mass-spring-capacitor system, machined
                out of silicon.  Left: the mechanism — the moving plate is
                the proof mass, suspended on springs, and its fingers
                interleave with fixed fingers to form capacitors that change
                as it moves.  Right: a micrograph of a real device's
                interleaved fingers, a few µm across.</caption>
                <sidebyside widths="66% 24%" margins="2%" valign="middle">
                    <image source="images/Day13x-Accelerometer/mems_mechanism.png"/>
                    <image source="images/Day13x-Accelerometer/mems_micrograph.png"/>
                </sidebyside>
            </figure>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13x-mems" ref="fig-accel-mems" stack="yes">
    <ul>
        <li>This is a MEMS accelerometer — a Micro-Electro-Mechanical System: the proof mass, the springs, and the capacitor plates are all machined out of silicon, on the scale of µm.</li>
        <li>A capacitance-to-voltage circuit on the same chip translates the changing capacitance into a voltage signal.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>≈ 1 min. Her slide 5 — the innovation of the last thirty years is putting the whole mass-spring system into silicon. The differential C1/C2 detail is in the chapter's Reference section; do not open it here.</note>
</slide>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 2 -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day13x-applications">
            <title>Part 2: Why Accelerometers Are Everywhere</title>

            <!-- DELIVERY 2: prose.  OWES: one paragraph carrying the three
                 examples and the one-sentence tail exactly as the slide has
                 them (Gate 1 spent this Part's slack — three examples plus
                 one sentence, no growth without a named displacement), and
                 the SignalQuest/Simbex sentence. -->

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13x-applications">
    <ul>
        <li>Tilt: your phone measures gravity's pull along each axis to decide when to rotate its screen.</li>
        <li>Shock: an airbag sensor detects the sudden deceleration of a crash.</li>
        <li>Free fall: before solid-state drives, a falling laptop pulled the disk heads off its spinning drive before impact.</li>
        <li>Accelerometers are also in wearables and football helmets, they monitor vibration in machinery, and paired with a gyroscope they form an IMU (inertial measurement unit) — the motion sensor in vehicles and drones.</li>
        <li>Two local companies founded by Thayer alumni build on exactly this sensor: SignalQuest (precision motion sensing) and Simbex (the helmet impact sensors).</li>
    </ul>
    <note>≈ 3 min, her slide 6 compressed to three examples plus one sentence (Gate 1's trade — the minutes went to Parts 1 and 4). If the clock is behind, this Part drops to one example.</note>
</slide>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 3 -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day13x-datasheet">
            <title>Part 3: Our Device, by Its Datasheet</title>

            <!-- DELIVERY 2: prose.  OWES: the lead-in paragraph from the
                 generic MEMS structure to our particular part (the LSM303AGR
                 we verified yesterday), a sentence for the block diagram's
                 chain, and the sensitivity lead-in that poses the commit's
                 question in the book's own words (P-18) ahead of
                 act-day13x-sensitivity; the modes-trade and zero-g
                 sentences following the reveal; the one-sentence breakout
                 close beside fig-accel-breakout. -->

            <figure xml:id="fig-lsm303agr-block">
                <caption>The LSM303AGR's block diagram, from its datasheet.
                The accelerometer is the upper half (boxed): three variable
                capacitors — one per axis — feed a charge amplifier, an
                analog-to-digital converter, and control logic that answers
                on the I2C bus (the SCL and SDA pins, boxed).  The lower
                half is a magnetometer, which we don't use; the chip also
                contains a temperature sensor.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day13x-Accelerometer/lsm303agr_block.svg" width="88%"/>
            </figure>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13x-block">
    <ul>
        <li>Our device: the STMicro LSM303AGR — a 3-axis accelerometer, plus a 3-axis magnetometer and a temperature sensor we won't use.</li>
        <li>Per axis, the chain is the one we just built: a variable capacitor, a charge amplifier, an analog-to-digital converter, and control logic on the I2C bus.</li>
        <li>You interact with it through control and data registers, over I2C (SPI is the other option; the breakout wires it for I2C by default).</li>
        <li>It also has hardware interrupt pins — INT_1_XL, INT_2_XL, and INT_MAG — which we're not using.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>≈ 1 min. Her slides 7 and 10, folded — the claims first; the diagram is next, full size, and the chain gets walked there. Registers over I2C is yesterday made general: WHO_AM_I was one of the control registers.</note>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13x-block-fig" ref="fig-lsm303agr-block">
    <caption>The accelerometer is the boxed upper half: find the three variable capacitors on the left, then the charge amplifier, the A/D converter, and the control logic that answers on the SCL and SDA pins.</caption>
    <note>≈ 2 min. Walk the chain left to right on the diagram — this is Part 1's physics arriving at yesterday's bus. The lower half is the magnetometer we don't use.</note>
</slide>

            <activity xml:id="act-day13x-sensitivity">
                <title>Derive the sensitivity</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>The datasheet's sensor-characteristics table
                    (<url href="external/datasheets/lsm303agr.pdf">LSM303AGR
                    datasheet</url>, §2.1, Table 3, pp. 13–14) is where the
                    measurement starts: first pick a full-scale range —
                    ±2 g, ±4 g, ±8 g, or ±16 g.  We'll use ±2 g, and the
                    sensor's <term>normal</term> resolution mode, which
                    gives 10 valid bits per reading.</p>
                </introduction>
                <task xml:id="task-day13x-sensitivity">
                    <statement><p>The <term>sensitivity</term> is the size of
                    one step of the output — how much acceleration one least
                    significant bit stands for.  From the range and the bit
                    count, compute it, in mg per step.  This is the ADC
                    argument from <xref ref="ch-adc"/> again: the full range,
                    divided into <m>2^{bits}</m> bins.</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13x-specs-commit" room="yes">
    <p>Pick the ±2 g range, in normal mode — 10 valid bits.  The sensitivity is the size of one step: how much acceleration does one least significant bit stand for?  Compute it, in mg.  (This is the ADC argument again: the full range, divided into 2^bits bins.)</p>
    <image source="images/Day13x-Accelerometer/accel_specs_masked.svg" width="82%"/>
    <note>≈ 3 min, committed in writing before the reveal.  The sensitivity column is masked on purpose — the datasheet prints the answer, and the point is to derive it (range / 2^bits, Day 7's 1 LSB = V_ref/2^B in new units).  Full range 4 g, not 2: the range is ±2 g.</note>
</slide>

            <instructor xml:id="ins-day13x-sensitivity">
                <p>The full range is 4 g — from −2 g to +2 g.  In normal
                mode there are 10 valid bits, so the range is divided into
                2¹⁰ = 1024 bins:
                4 g / 1024 = 3.90625 mg per step.  The datasheet's Table 3
                prints 3.9 mg/LSB for this range and mode — our derived
                number, rounded — and the note under the table says the same
                thing in its own words: <q>1 LSb = 3.9 mg in normal mode
                (10-bit) at FS = ±2 g</q>.  Rounded once more, one digit of
                the output is worth about 4 mg.</p>
            </instructor>

            <figure xml:id="fig-accel-specs">
                <caption>The sensor-characteristics table's sensitivity and
                zero-g offset rows (LSM303AGR datasheet §2.1, Table 3,
                pp. 13–14).  Top: the full-scale range rows.  Middle: one
                sensitivity row per range and resolution mode —
                high-resolution is 12 bits, normal 10, low-power 8 — and at
                ±2 g in normal mode the table prints 3.9 mg/LSB, the
                number we derived as 4 g / 2¹⁰ = 3.90625 mg.  Bottom: the
                zero-g offset row — a sensor lying perfectly flat can read
                up to ±40 mg away from the true value.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day13x-Accelerometer/accel_specs_range.png" width="86%"/>
                <image source="images/Day13x-Accelerometer/accel_specs_sensitivity.svg" width="86%"/>
                <image source="images/Day13x-Accelerometer/accel_specs_offset.png" width="86%"/>
            </figure>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13x-specs-reveal">
    <ul>
        <li>The table prints 3.9 mg/LSB at ±2 g in normal mode — our 3.90625, rounded.  The three modes trade speed against resolution: fewer bits means faster measurements and bigger steps.</li>
        <li>The zero-g offset row (below) is worth reading honestly: lying perfectly flat, the sensor can read up to ±40 mg away from the true value — that is ten of our steps.</li>
    </ul>
    <image source="images/Day13x-Accelerometer/accel_specs_sensitivity.svg" width="68%"/>
    <image source="images/Day13x-Accelerometer/accel_specs_offset.png" width="68%"/>
    <note>≈ 1 min — the unmasked column confirms the arithmetic; then the modes trade and the offset row. ±40 mg is significant — it comes back as the stretch's calibration question, and tomorrow's suggested tests will show it on real boards.</note>
</slide>

            <figure xml:id="fig-accel-breakout">
                <caption>The breakout board's schematic (partial).  The
                pull-up resistors for SDA and SCL are on the board, and
                pull-ups on the chip's configuration pins strap it into I2C
                mode; a regulator supplies the chip, and the 6-pin header
                is where the STEMMA QT connector's four wires arrive.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day13x-Accelerometer/breakout_schematic.svg" width="92%"/>
            </figure>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13x-breakout" ref="fig-accel-breakout">
    <caption>The breakout does the analog housekeeping for us: pull-ups on the board, a regulator, and pull-ups that strap the chip into I2C mode.</caption>
    <note>≈ 1 min, one sentence — her slide 9.  The big chip is the accelerometer, the little one the regulator; the SDA and SCL wires run to on-board pull-ups.  Do not linger; the schematic is here so the board is not a black box.</note>
</slide>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 4 — the CRUCIAL step (plans/day13x.md): staged one idea at
             a time; nobody meets left-justification, sign, and scaling in
             the same breath. -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day13x-data-format">
            <title>Part 4: The Data Format</title>

            <!-- DELIVERY 2: prose.  OWES: the subaddress weld sentence as
                 prose (the slide asserts it; the paragraph retells it — the
                 datasheet's own word for yesterday's register address); the
                 one-byte-unsigned paragraph with the 0x4000 walk; the sign
                 paragraph (bit 15 is worth −2¹⁵ — FIRST teaching, nothing
                 before Week 7 decodes a signed bit pattern; the 4-bit +2/−2
                 contrast; the 0xC000 worked example); the collapse
                 paragraph reading fig-accel-data-format; and the lead-in
                 sentences for the auto-increment and byte-assembly beats
                 (their load-bearing content is already written below). -->

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13x-one-byte">
    <ul>
        <li>The acceleration readings arrive in the data registers, at a <term>subaddress</term> — the datasheet's own word for the register address we used yesterday.</li>
        <li>Each axis's reading is 16 bits — two bytes — and it is <term>left-justified</term>: the valid bits are at the top of the word, and zeros fill in below.</li>
        <li>In low-power mode there are 8 valid bits.  Take the reading 0x4000: the top byte is 0x40 = 64.  Each step is 4 g / 2⁸ = 15.625 mg, so this reading is 64 × 15.625 mg = 1000 mg — one g.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>≈ 3 min, one idea at a time: subaddress weld, then left-justified, then one byte unsigned — shift down, scale by the step size.  No sign yet and no three-mode picture yet, on purpose.  0x4000 → +1000 mg is the number the flip example will mirror.</note>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13x-sign">
    <ul>
        <li>So far we read the byte as unsigned — but acceleration has a direction, and the sensor's output is a <term>two's complement</term> number: bit 15 is worth −2¹⁵, not +2¹⁵.  Every other bit keeps its usual value.</li>
        <li>The idea in 4 bits: 0b0010 reads 2.  In two's complement, 0b1110 reads −8 + 4 + 2 = −2 — the top bit is worth −8, and the rest are ordinary.</li>
        <li>A number with its top bit set is therefore negative, and its value is the unsigned reading minus 2 to the width: 0b1110 is 14 − 16 = −2.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>≈ 3 min — FIRST teaching, not review: nothing before this week has decoded a signed bit pattern.  Land the one fact (the top bit's value is negative) and its consequence (unsigned minus 2^width), both shown in 4 bits before anyone sees 16.</note>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13x-worked">
    <ul>
        <li>The reading is 0xC000 = 0b1100 0000 0000 0000.  Bit 15 is set, so the value is negative: −2¹⁵ + 2¹⁴ = −32768 + 16384 = −16384.  (Equivalently: unsigned 0xC000 is 49152, and 49152 − 65536 = −16384.)</li>
        <li>Carried to milli-g: (4 × −16384 × 1000) / 2¹⁶ = <em>−1000 mg</em> — minus one g.</li>
        <li>That is tomorrow's experiment: a board lying flat reads about +1000 mg on its z axis, and flipping it over flips the sign.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>≈ 2 min, worked TOGETHER on the board, top to bottom — this is the model for the conversion the room does alone next.  Both sign routes shown once: the bit-worth sum and unsigned-minus-2¹⁶.</note>
</slide>

            <figure xml:id="fig-accel-data-format">
                <caption>The three resolution modes write their valid bits
                into the same 16-bit word, always starting at bit 15 —
                low-power mode fills 8 bits, normal 10, high-resolution 12,
                with zeros below.  Bit 15 is the sign, worth −2¹⁵.  Because
                the valid bits always start in the same place, one formula
                converts the raw word in every mode:
                <m>a = \mathrm{FS} \cdot \mathrm{raw} / 2^{16}</m>.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day13x-Accelerometer/data_format_rows.svg" width="80%"/>
            </figure>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13x-collapse" ref="fig-accel-data-format" stack="yes">
    <ul>
        <li>In each mode: shift the zeros off, then scale by that mode's step.  Low-power: (raw ≫ 8) · 4g/2⁸.  Normal: (raw ≫ 6) · 4g/2¹⁰.  High-resolution: (raw ≫ 4) · 4g/2¹².</li>
        <li>Every one of them simplifies to the same thing: a = 4g · raw / 2¹⁶.  Left-justification is why — the valid bits always start at bit 15, so the shift and the step size always cancel to 2¹⁶.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>≈ 2 min. Her slide 15's three bit-rows, with the zeros visible — show the algebra on one row and say the other two collapse the same way.  The punchline is one formula in every mode.</note>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13x-mg-formula">
    <p>In milli-g, for the ±2 g range, the conversion is</p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
a = (4 * ACCEL_raw * 1000) / 65536             // in mg

// or, with names instead of magic numbers:
#define ACC_FS            4                    // full-scale range, in g
#define ACC_REGISTERWIDTH 16
#define MILLI             1000

a = (ACC_FS * ACCEL_raw * MILLI) >> ACC_REGISTERWIDTH;
]]></code></program>
    <caption>The units of the output are g — 1 g = 9.8 m/s² — so multiplying by 1000 gives milli-g, and the divide by 2¹⁶ is the shift.</caption>
    <note>≈ 1 min, her slide 16.  The #defines are accel_test.c's, verbatim; tomorrow's scaling line in accel_test.c is this computation, done once per axis.</note>
</slide>

            <activity xml:id="act-day13x-convert">
                <title>Convert a reading</title>
                <task xml:id="task-day13x-convert">
                    <statement><p>The sensor is on the ±2 g range and the
                    raw reading is 0xE000.  On paper, convert it to
                    milli-g: write out the binary, decide the sign, compute
                    the value, and carry it to mg.  Then check your answer
                    for plausibility — is it inside the range?</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day13x-offset-stretch">
                    <statement><p>If you finish early: the datasheet's
                    zero-g offset row says a flat, motionless board can
                    read up to ±40 mg off.  Suppose your board reads
                    +1032 mg lying flat and −968 mg lying flipped.  What is
                    its offset, and how would you correct future
                    readings?</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>

            <instructor xml:id="ins-day13x-convert">
                <p>0xE000 = 0b1110 0000 0000 0000.  Bit 15 is set, so the
                value is negative: −2¹⁵ + 2¹⁴ + 2¹³ = −32768 + 16384 + 8192
                = −8192 (equivalently, 57344 − 65536 = −8192).  In mg:
                (4 × −8192 × 1000) / 65536 = <em>−500 mg</em>.  Plausible —
                well inside ±2000 mg.</p>
                <p>The stretch: the two readings should be +1000 and
                −1000 mg; they are both 32 mg high, so the offset is their
                average, (1032 + (−968)) / 2 = +32 mg.  Correct future
                readings by subtracting 32 mg.  That is the calibration
                her datasheet note describes: read flat, read flipped,
                average.</p>
            </instructor>

            <!-- Auto-increment: the load-bearing quote is written now
                 (the slide asserts it); B-8a. -->
            <p>
                The three axes arrive as six data registers at successive
                subaddresses — <c>OUT_X_L_A</c> at 0x28 through
                <c>OUT_Z_H_A</c> at 0x2D — and you can read all six bytes
                in one transfer, starting at 0x28.  One thing has to be
                said to the chip first, and the datasheet says it in §6.1.1
                (p. 38):
            </p>
            <blockquote>
                <p>After the start condition (ST) a slave address is sent,
                once a slave acknowledge (SAK) has been returned, an 8-bit
                sub-address (SUB) is transmitted: the 7 LSb represent the
                actual register address while the MSB enables address auto
                increment.  If the MSb of the SUB field is <sq>1</sq>, the
                SUB (register address) is automatically increased to allow
                multiple data read/writes.</p>
            </blockquote>
            <p>
                So instead of sending 0x28 as the register address, send
                <c>0x28 | (1&lt;&lt;7)</c>: the accelerometer then advances
                to the next data register after every byte, and one
                six-byte read collects all three axes.
            </p>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13x-autoincrement">
    <p>The three axes arrive as six data registers at successive subaddresses — OUT_X_L_A at 0x28 through OUT_Z_H_A at 0x2D — and you can read all six bytes in one transfer.  But note (Datasheet §6.1.1): the subaddress's MSB enables address auto-increment.  Send <c>0x28 | (1&lt;&lt;7)</c>, and the register address advances after every byte.</p>
    <image source="images/Day13-I2C(3)/i2c_transfer_reads.svg" width="88%"/>
    <note>≈ 2 min. The tables are yesterday's — Table 22 is the WHO_AM_I read's shape; Table 23 is today's payoff, the multi-byte read: after each DATA the controller ACKs (MAK) and the chip serves the next register, until the final NACK.  The gloss line under Table 23 is the auto-increment rule.</note>
</slide>

            <!-- Byte assembly: its own beat (Gate 1 item 4) — the claim is
                 load-bearing for Lab 7 D1, so the paragraph is written
                 now. -->
            <p>
                The six bytes come back <em>low byte first</em>:
                <c>OUT_X_L_A</c> at 0x28 holds the low byte of x and
                <c>OUT_X_H_A</c> at 0x29 the high byte.
                <c>lsm303_AccelReadRaw()</c> — given to you, and short
                enough to read whole — makes the six-byte read and then
                assembles each pair into one signed 16-bit value:
                <c>((int16_t) data[1] &lt;&lt; 8) | ((int16_t) data[0])</c>
                promotes each byte to 16 bits, moves the high byte up
                eight places, and ORs the two together.  This is a
                different use of the shift-and-OR machinery than we have
                seen so far: it builds a wider signed value out of two
                array elements, rather than setting a bit inside a
                register.
            </p>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13x-readraw">
    <p><c>lsm303agr.h</c> declares a structure for one three-axis reading, and <c>lsm303_AccelReadRaw()</c> — given to you — fills it: one six-byte auto-increment read, then each pair of bytes is assembled low-byte-first into a signed 16-bit value.</p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
typedef struct {
	int16_t x;
	int16_t y;
	int16_t z;
} lsm303AccelData_s;

void lsm303_AccelReadRaw(lsm303AccelData_s *result) {
	uint8_t data[6];

	i2c1_memRead(LSM303_ADDRESS_ACCEL, LSM303_OUT_X_L_A | (1<<7), 6, &data[0]);

	result->x = ((int16_t) data[1] << 8) | ((int16_t) data[0]);
	result->y = ((int16_t) data[3] << 8) | ((int16_t) data[2]);
	result->z = ((int16_t) data[5] << 8) | ((int16_t) data[4]);
}
]]></code></program>
    <caption>These functions use i2c.c and lsm303agr.h — Day 10's firmware layers, made concrete.</caption>
    <note>≈ 2 min.  The listing is abridged for the wall (the file's comment blocks are off it); the full file arrives tomorrow as lsm303agr_partial.c.  Low byte first: data[0] is OUT_X_L_A.  The shift-and-OR builds a wider signed value from two array elements — a new use of familiar machinery, worth saying out loud.</note>
</slide>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 5 — her slide 11, deliberately re-ordered to the end
             (Gate 1): the control-register question is the last thing said
             before the homework that answers it.  NOT cuttable — it is the
             sole in-class setup for the homework Day 14's opening commit
             stands on. -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day13x-control-registers">
            <title>Part 5: What Is Left to Configure</title>

            <!-- DELIVERY 2: prose.  OWES: the lead-in paragraph (the data
                 format is settled; what remains is telling the sensor how
                 to run) and a closing sentence pointing at the reading,
                 xref sec-accel-day14-before. -->

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13x-control-regs">
    <p>The accelerometer has six control registers, all reachable over I2C.  Only two of them matter for us — the rest are about features we leave at their defaults.</p>
    <tabular halign="left">
        <col width="30%"/><col width="70%"/>
        <row header="yes"><cell>Register</cell><cell>What it governs</cell></row>
        <row><cell><c>CTRL_REG1_A</c></cell><cell>Data rate, power mode, axis selection — <em>ours to set</em>.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><c>CTRL_REG2_A</c></cell><cell>Analog filter selection (we accept the defaults).</cell></row>
        <row><cell><c>CTRL_REG3_A</c></cell><cell>Interrupt selection (none used; off by default).</cell></row>
        <row><cell><c>CTRL_REG4_A</c></cell><cell>Full-scale range (±2, ±4, ±8, ±16 g) and resolution mode — <em>ours to set</em>.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><c>CTRL_REG5_A</c></cell><cell>More interrupts (none used; off by default).</cell></row>
        <row><cell><c>CTRL_REG6_A</c></cell><cell>Yet more interrupts (none used; off by default).</cell></row>
    </tabular>
    <note>≈ 3 min, her slide 11 moved to the end on purpose: this question is the last thing said before the homework that answers it.  Everything today assumed ±2 g and normal mode — CTRL_REG1_A and CTRL_REG4_A are where those choices actually get made.  End-of-class beat: protected, do not cut.</note>
</slide>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Close -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day13x-homework">
            <title>Homework for Thursday</title>

            <!-- DELIVERY 2: prose.  OWES: the two-sentence close (the
                 scavenger-hunt reading, xref sec-accel-day14-before,
                 decides what goes into those two registers; RegisterWrite
                 on paper if not already done; tomorrow, data on the
                 screen). -->

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13x-close">
    <ul>
        <li>Do the accelerometer readings on the daily page — the reading is a scavenger hunt through the datasheet, and it decides what goes into CTRL_REG1_A and CTRL_REG4_A.  Take notes on paper.</li>
        <li>On paper, write <c>lsm303_AccelRegisterWrite()</c>, if you have not already.  (Mimic the read function.)</li>
    </ul>
    <caption>Tomorrow we'll put your settings into the sensor and get acceleration data onto the screen.</caption>
    <note>≈ 2 min, her slide 19.  Same assignment as yesterday's Part 8 — this is the reminder, not a new item.  The register question is the one the class was just set up for.</note>
</slide>
        </subsection>
